INT. COUNTY PAROLE OFFICE - WAITING AREA - MORNING

Fluorescent lights. A row of molded chairs. A ficus that died years ago and has not been told.

Deb, 60s, reception, bifocals, a mug that says WORLD'S OKAYEST GRANDMA, taps at a keyboard behind scratched plexiglass. She doesn't look up when the door opens.

A man comes in. He is tall, late forties, a gray work jacket zipped to the throat, a folder pressed to his ribs like a shield. He stands very still just inside the door, as if the room might reject him. THE MAN waits.

DEB
Name?

THE MAN
I have a nine o'clock.

DEB
Name.

He says nothing for a second. She finally looks at him.

THE MAN
Is Officer Okafor in?

DEB
Honey, everybody's in. It's nine in the morning. Sit.

He sits. He keeps the folder on his knees. Across the room, a clock ticks, slower than a clock should.

INT. COUNTY PAROLE OFFICE - OFFICER OKAFOR'S OFFICE - LATER

Ruth Okafor, 50s, reading glasses pushed up into gray braids, sits behind a desk piled with files, a cactus, a photograph of a boy in a graduation gown turned slightly away from the visitor's chair. She is calm, almost bored. She has done this four thousand times.

The man sits. He puts the folder on the desk.

RUTH
Mr. Voss. How's the job?

THE MAN
Fine.

RUTH
The apartment?

THE MAN
Fine.

RUTH
You've been fine for six weeks. I'm starting to think you're a fine person.

He doesn't smile. She turns a page.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Says here you missed your Thursday check-in.

THE MAN
I called.

RUTH
You left a message with Deb. Deb doesn't write things down. Deb is a lovely woman and a terrible secretary.

THE MAN
I was at work. They put me on a double.

RUTH
Did you bring a pay stub?

He slides the folder across. She opens it, flips, and stops. Her eyebrows lift very slightly.

RUTH (CONT'D)
This isn't a pay stub.

It's a photograph. A school photo, a boy of about eight, missing a front tooth. She looks at it a long time. When she looks up, her voice is lower.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Where did you get this?

THE MAN
Mailbox. Yesterday.

RUTH
It's not allowed. You know it's not allowed. The order says no contact.

THE MAN
I didn't contact anybody. It came in the mail. I didn't do a thing.

He leans forward. For the first time his voice shakes, not much, just enough.

THE MAN (CONT'D)
Somebody wants me to violate. That's all this is. Somebody wants me back inside.

RUTH
Who?

He doesn't answer. His hands, on his knees, have begun to tremble, and he presses them flat.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Mr. Voss, I need a name.

THE MAN
Calvin.

RUTH
Excuse me?

THE MAN
Calvin. My name's Calvin. Everybody calls me Voss like I'm a case number. I'd like, once, for somebody in this building to say it.

She sets the photograph down, gently, and for a moment the whole office seems to hold its breath. Deb's keyboard has stopped. Somewhere a printer jams.

RUTH
Calvin.

He closes his eyes.

RUTH (CONT'D)
I'm going to make a call. You're going to sit right here. You are not in trouble.

CALVIN
(opening his eyes)
That's what they said last time.

She stands, takes the photograph, and walks to the door, and in the doorway she hesitates, and does not look back, and this is the last time the two of them will sit in the same room as strangers.
